# March 22, 2026: Introduction and First CAD

<div align="center">

The ALL-bot is designed to solve the problem of robots being fragile and not modular[cite: 85]. I started the CAD drawing by focusing on the sprocket wheels (thorns to guide the tracks)[cite: 84]. I chose two thin sheets of 1cm thickness with a 5cm radius, combined with tension wheels of 1.5cm thickness and 2.5cm radius[cite: 84]. I also modeled the body with a plan to have two internal floors: bottom for motors/battery, top for the microcontroller[cite: 84]. 

# April 9, 2026: Chassis and Tracks

<div align="center">

I faced massive issues designing the track links. The first two versions failed because of the thorn hole measurements and a texture that was too tall and fragile[cite: 70, 71]. The final link version uses a 0.5mm clearance on each side, V-shapes for adherence on soft materials, and larger 3.3mm radius holes for stronger pins[cite: 72]. I also hollowed out the chassis into a "skeleton style" to save filament and look cooler[cite: 72]. I designed a casing for the ultrasonic sensors and added an access hole at the rear panel[cite: 65, 66].

# April 16, 2026: Mechanical Pulleys and CAD Finish

<div align="center">

I discovered a critical issue: the NEMA 17 shafts were too short, forcing me to use mechanical couplers which increases cost and lessens durability[cite: 51]. To eliminate direct drive and increase torque for the heavy chassis, I introduced a 15-tooth to 60-tooth pulley system with a 180mm belt[cite: 41, 42, 43]. This increased the torque by a 1:4 ratio[cite: 43]. However, this destroyed my internal space, forcing me to delete the central horizontal support pillar[cite: 42, 46]. Finally, I designed a hexagonal grill top plate for cooling and modular mounting[cite: 38].

# April 22, 2026: Electronics Schematics

<div align="center">

I split the electronics into two boards. The **Sensor Board** handles the micro-controller, IMU (BNO055), temperature sensor (LM75C), and a voltage divider for battery capacity[cite: 30]. The **Power Board** handles the 11.1V to 5V (MP2307) and 3.3V (AMS1117) step-downs, TMC2209 drivers, and an XT60 battery connector[cite: 31, 33]. I added a 6A fuse and a P-channel mosfet for short-circuit and reverse polarity protection[cite: 27]. I had to switch from a ribbon cable to an XT30 connector between the boards because the NEMA 17 motors draw 2A each, which would have melted the ribbon[cite: 25].

# May 21, 2026: PCB Routing and Final Boards

<div align="center">

I routed the Sensor Board first. I had to downgrade from a 4-layer PCB to a 2-layer PCB, ensuring I kept signals and power spaced out[cite: 10]. The DRC flagged 91 violations initially due to clearance issues between high-current routes and the MCU/IMU[cite: 15, 16]. After correcting the spacing, I generated the Gerber files for JLCPCB, though I noticed an inventory shortage for some components like the 470µF capacitor[cite: 16]. I then completed the Power Board with the buck regulator and TMC2209 sockets[cite: 6].
